# Nothing is routed through Git LFS any more.
#
# The repository used to keep the two Android V8 archives (~247 MB together) in
# LFS. Every CI checkout spent that much LFS bandwidth, the quota ran out, and
# checkout then failed for a reason unrelated to any change being tested -- the
# build job was red for days with `batch response: This repository exceeded its
# LFS budget`. Release assets do not count against that quota, so the archives
# now live on a release and `scripts/fetch-v8-archives.sh` pulls them, verifying
# each against the `hashes.archive` sha256 in the committed
# component-manifest.json. That is a stronger guarantee than LFS gave: LFS proved
# the bytes matched an object id, but nothing tied that object to the manifest
# the release gate trusts.
#
# The V8 startup snapshots stayed behind as ordinary blobs. At 1.4-2.1 MB each
# they are far below the size where LFS earns its keep, and keeping them plain
# means the repository has no LFS dependency at all -- so no future quota can
# take CI down again. They are still committed (release builds are hermetic, and
# the arm64 snapshot has no other home because hosted CI cannot generate it), and
# scripts/check-snapshot-freshness.sh still enforces that they match the tree.
#
# The V8 archives themselves are git-ignored build products; see .gitignore.
# If a large binary genuinely needs tracking here again, prefer a release asset
# plus a checksum in a manifest over reintroducing LFS.

# Published vendor archives, ordered patches and generated FFI bindings are
# checked byte for byte. core.autocrlf on a Windows checkout must not rewrite
# source/license inputs after their hashes have been verified.
/engine/third_party/deno_core-*/** -text
/engine/third_party/v8-*/** -text
/engine/third_party/ada-url-*/** -text
/engine/third_party/skia-bindings-*/** -text
/engine/third_party/rustls-platform-verifier-android-*/** -text
/engine/third_party/*-patches/** -text
/engine/third_party/rusty_v8/*/src_binding.rs -text
